{
  "reports": [
    {
      "title": "Triac gate connection failure disables cooling fan group in synthetic substation",
      "core_units": [
        {
          "text": "The triac gate terminal screw had engaged only two threads, creating high resistance in the gate drive path.",
          "role": "root_cause",
          "causal_predecessors": []
        },
        {
          "text": "At 13:20 on 8 September 2026, Synthetic Substation set a new ambient temperature record of 44.2 °C.",
          "role": "trigger_event",
          "causal_predecessors": []
        },
        {
          "text": "To satisfy area dispatch, the transformer remained loaded to 128 MVA, which was 13 MVA above its forced-air rating.",
          "role": "trigger_event",
          "causal_predecessors": []
        },
        {
          "text": "The earlier cooling-control modification removed the fan current relay, so a start command and actual running status were the same logic point.",
          "role": "root_cause",
          "causal_predecessors": []
        },
        {
          "text": "A range shift in the SCADA oil-temperature transmitter, introduced by an isolator card replacement in June, made it report 14 °C low.",
          "role": "root_cause",
          "causal_predecessors": []
        },
        {
          "text": "A stationary storm cell held wind speed below 1.1 m/s all afternoon, suppressing natural convection from the transformer tanks.",
          "role": "trigger_event",
          "causal_predecessors": []
        },
        {
          "text": "An operator had changed the high-ambient alarm setpoint from 40 °C to 45 °C, so the 44.2 °C event did not alarm.",
          "role": "trigger_event",
          "causal_predecessors": []
        },
        {
          "text": "The triac's gate circuit expected at least 25 mA; the controller could deliver only 12 mA when the loose connection raised that path's resistance.",
          "role": "root_cause",
          "causal_predecessors": [0]
        },
        {
          "text": "At 13:38 the controller saw the true oil temperature at 70 °C and issued a start command to fan group B.",
          "role": "propagation_or_response",
          "causal_predecessors": [1, 2, 5]
        },
        {
          "text": "Because the triac never conducted, the fan motor drew 0 A, yet the panel indicated 'on' because the command voltage existed.",
          "role": "propagation_or_response",
          "causal_predecessors": [8, 0, 3, 7]
        },
        {
          "text": "With group B idle, top-oil temperature climbed from 70 °C to 93 °C in 39 minutes, while the SCADA display never left 68 °C.",
          "role": "propagation_or_response",
          "causal_predecessors": [9, 4]
        },
        {
          "text": "At 14:18 the independent winding RTD exceeded 124 °C, starting a 5-s timer that energized the 86 lockout relay, tripping the 52 breakers.",
          "role": "propagation_or_response",
          "causal_predecessors": [10]
        },
        {
          "text": "The transformer was separated at 14:18; area automatic underfrequency shedding removed 47 MW, and full feeder restoration completed at 15:13.",
          "role": "impact",
          "causal_predecessors": [11]
        },
        {
          "text": "Applying the IEEE C57.91 model to the observed 131 °C hotspot for 14 minutes gives a life loss of 4.3 equivalent hours.",
          "role": "impact",
          "causal_predecessors": [10, 11]
        },
        {
          "text": "No overcurrent or differential relay picked up, so the outage and load loss are accredited solely to the cooling-control failure.",
          "role": "impact",
          "causal_predecessors": [11]
        },
        {
          "text": "The forced outage of the transformer lasted 18 hours, during which 128 MVA of import capability was missing from the synthetic grid.",
          "role": "impact",
          "causal_predecessors": [12]
        },
        {
          "text": "Technicians replaced the gate wire lug and screw, cleaned the oxide, and torqued the terminal block to the specified 1.2 N·m.",
          "role": "mitigation",
          "causal_predecessors": [0, 7]
        },
        {
          "text": "A current transformer was installed in the fan branch; control logic now requires a measured current above 5 A before showing fan on.",
          "role": "mitigation",
          "causal_predecessors": [3]
        },
        {
          "text": "The SCADA transmitter is now calibrated against a known reference RTD, and a full linearization check is completed every year.",
          "role": "mitigation",
          "causal_predecessors": [4]
        },
        {
          "text": "Synthetic substation adopted quarterly start/challenge tests for all cooler groups and a monthly infrared scan of triac terminals.",
          "role": "mitigation",
          "causal_predecessors": [0, 3, 7]
        }
      ],
      "distractor_seeds": [
        "A radiator fan setpoint of 70 °C is common, but some designs use 60 °C to allow for hotspots.",
        "Synthetic Substation's transformer cooling system has six radiators arranged on each side of the tank.",
        "Cooling fan motors are often protected with thermal overload relays that do not require separate current transformers.",
        "The top-oil temperature rise over ambient is a measure of transformer loading and cooling effectiveness.",
        "In an OA/FA transformer, the nameplate rating indicates the maximum load with and without forced air cooling.",
        "When a fan group is first started, the motor draws an inrush current of up to eight times the full-load value.",
        "Transformer cooling pumps are not used in all substations; many designs rely on natural oil convection only.",
        "A spare fan motor can be mounted on a common frame and easily exchanged if a major mechanical defect arises.",
        "The preferred setting for a winding temperature alarm is 90 °C for a 65 °C-rise transformer.",
        "Heat wave conditions can cause an increase in the amount of solar radiation that reaches the radiator surfaces.",
        "The tap changer control does not have authority over cooling bank loading.",
        "An infrared camera can be used to scan a live transformer for uneven thermal signatures on the tank."
      ]
    },
    {
      "title": "Firmware underflow causes cooling fan bearing failure in synthetic substation transformer",
      "core_units": [
        {
          "text": "Firmware v4.12, installed on 1 July, had an integer underflow that reset fan group B's start setpoint to −273 °C.",
          "role": "root_cause",
          "causal_predecessors": []
        },
        {
          "text": "The triac gate was asserted every half-cycle without an off state, so group B's fans ran continuously since that firmware date.",
          "role": "root_cause",
          "causal_predecessors": [0]
        },
        {
          "text": "Because the fan breaker auxiliary position was never wired in, the controller could not tell whether the actual contact was open or closed.",
          "role": "root_cause",
          "causal_predecessors": []
        },
        {
          "text": "The fan motor bearing had reached 22,000 running hours and was due for replacement in the operator's maintenance plan, but it was omitted.",
          "role": "root_cause",
          "causal_predecessors": []
        },
        {
          "text": "At 14:00 on 8 September 2026, Synthetic Substation reached 44.2 °C ambient and solar gain stayed high at the transformer.",
          "role": "trigger_event",
          "causal_predecessors": []
        },
        {
          "text": "Load dispatcher raised the transformer load to 129 MVA at 14:10, above the rating that requires all fans.",
          "role": "trigger_event",
          "causal_predecessors": []
        },
        {
          "text": "Thunderstorm at 13:45 left little wind; by 14:00 wind speed had dropped to 0.4 m/s.",
          "role": "trigger_event",
          "causal_predecessors": []
        },
        {
          "text": "At 14:05 the transformer oil top thermometer passed 70 °C, which according to the station manual requires both fan groups.",
          "role": "trigger_event",
          "causal_predecessors": [4, 5]
        },
        {
          "text": "At 15:10 the fan motor bearing locked, causing locked-rotor current of 48 amperes.",
          "role": "propagation_or_response",
          "causal_predecessors": [1, 3]
        },
        {
          "text": "After three seconds, the motor thermal overload relay opened the dedicated 52 breaker to de-energize the fan motor.",
          "role": "propagation_or_response",
          "causal_predecessors": [8]
        },
        {
          "text": "With the fan out and the controller not sensing breaker position, the triac continued to gate and top-oil rose to 98 °C.",
          "role": "propagation_or_response",
          "causal_predecessors": [2, 9]
        },
        {
          "text": "At 15:35, the winding temperature relay timed out, energizing the 86 lockout relay, which tripped the main 52 breakers.",
          "role": "propagation_or_response",
          "causal_predecessors": [10]
        },
        {
          "text": "The transformer was isolated at 15:35, dropping 129 MVA to a group of industrial feeders for the next 46 minutes.",
          "role": "impact",
          "causal_predecessors": [11]
        },
        {
          "text": "Hot-spot temperature was estimated at 126 °C for 26 minutes, which per IEEE model yields 1.2 equivalent hours of lost life.",
          "role": "impact",
          "causal_predecessors": [10, 11]
        },
        {
          "text": "The station report shows no overcurrent, no differential relay, and no sudden-pressure relay operation; the event came entirely from cooling loss.",
          "role": "impact",
          "causal_predecessors": [11]
        },
        {
          "text": "A subsequent insulation test at 1 kV was normal, but the fan bay stayed out for eleven days with the transformer back in service.",
          "role": "impact",
          "causal_predecessors": [9]
        },
        {
          "text": "Firmware was rolled back to the previous validated release and a lower saturation limit added in the temperature-setpoint routine.",
          "role": "mitigation",
          "causal_predecessors": [0]
        },
        {
          "text": "A hard-wired 52 auxiliary contact was connected to the controller; the triac gate is now inhibited when the breaker is open.",
          "role": "mitigation",
          "causal_predecessors": [2]
        },
        {
          "text": "The fan motor was overhauled, a new bearing installed, and the cooling group's phase-loss relay was adjusted to a sensitive level.",
          "role": "mitigation",
          "causal_predecessors": [3, 8]
        },
        {
          "text": "Synthetic substation now requires a bearing remaining-life check every 5,000 operating hours for every cooler fan motor.",
          "role": "mitigation",
          "causal_predecessors": [3]
        }
      ],
      "distractor_seeds": [
        "The cooling controller in some designs uses a microprocessor with a real-time clock and historical data log.",
        "A fan motor's current signature reflects its load torque and may indicate mechanical wear before the bearing seizes.",
        "The lockout relay (ANSI 86) is used in protection schemes to require manual reset after a trip.",
        "A circuit breaker (ANSI 52) can be used to switch both main power and auxiliary motor circuits.",
        "Triacs are sensitive to voltage transients and often protected by snubber circuits across the MT1–MT2 terminals.",
        "Transformer cooling may be classified as OA (oil-air), FA (oil-air forced), and directed air forced.",
        "Forced-air cooling adds 20-35% to the transformer's capacity, depending on the bracing and fan efficiency.",
        "Maintenance intervals for fan bearings depend on the running hours, not only on calendar time.",
        "An oil temperature indicator on a transformer is typically a mechanical dial connected to a temperature well.",
        "The ambient temperature during a heat wave can increase the viscosity of the insulating oil.",
        "Transformer fans are usually arranged in banks, with each bank controlled by a dedicated contactor.",
        "A cooling bank may be manually forced on, even during low-load seasons, to check for fan failures."
      ]
    }
  ]
}
